<comment datatype="string">&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=ENZYME&amp;object=Radical-AdoMet-Enzymes"&gt;Radical-SAM (&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-adenosyl-L-methionine) enzymes&lt;/a&gt;, often referred to as radical-AdoMet enzymes, catalyze various types of reactions utilizing the radical generated by the cleavage of an &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-adenosyl-L-methionine&lt;/a&gt; (AdoMet or SAM) molecule. The reaction starts when an electron supplied by a flavodoxin domain is transferred to an iron-sulfur cluster, shifting the cluster to its most reduced state (for example, [4Fe&amp;ndash;4S]&lt;sup&gt;1+&lt;/sup&gt;). The electron is then transferred to a bound &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-adenosyl-L-methionine&lt;/a&gt; molecule that interacts with the FeS cluster, resulting in its reductive cleavage to a &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-15400"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosin-5'-yl radical&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=MET"&gt;L-methionine&lt;/a&gt;. The reactive 5'-deoxyadenosyl radical abstracts a hydrogen atom from the substrate, prompting the actual reaction to start. In the process the radical is converted to &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;p&gt;
Accumulation of &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt; leads to defects in growth and metabolism [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19328201"&gt;Challand09&lt;/a&gt;]. On the other hand, elimination via export constitutes a loss of valuable organic carbon and nitrogen.
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
The bacteria &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1085"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Rhodospirillum rubrum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1076"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Rhodopseudomonas palustris&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; possess three genes (&lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15516"&gt;mtnP&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15512"&gt;mtnA&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-80668"&gt;ald2&lt;/a&gt;) that encode enzymes that catalyze the initial steps of methionine salvage pathways from &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=5-METHYLTHIOADENOSINE"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5'-thioadenosine&lt;/a&gt;. Those pathways proceed via &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-23221"&gt;(2-methylsulfanyl)acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt; as the key product, with &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE"&gt;glycerone phosphate&lt;/a&gt; produced as a side product [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29133429"&gt;North17&lt;/a&gt;] (see &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-8132"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5-thio-&amp;alpha;-D-ribose 1-phosphate degradation III&lt;/a&gt;).
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;i&gt;In vitro&lt;/i&gt; studies of these enzymes indicated that they may also function in &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt; metabolism. To investigate this possibility, individual deletions of the three genes were constructed in &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1085"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Rhodospirillum rubrum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. All three mutants accumulated &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt; extracellularly to a level twofold higher than that of the wild-type strain. Plasmid-based gene complementation of each respective gene deletion strain with the relevant gene restored &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt; abundance to wild-type level. Thus the three enzymes participate in an oxygen-independent dual-purpose metabolic pathway that can degrade both &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=5-METHYLTHIOADENOSINE"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5'-thioadenosine&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31950558"&gt;North20&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
Labeling of &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH33ADO"&gt;5'-deoxyadenosine&lt;/a&gt; with tritium identified the products of the Ald2 aldolase as &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETALD"&gt;acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE"&gt;glycerone phosphate&lt;/a&gt;. Further confirmation of the proposed enzyme activites was achieved by production of purified recombinant proteins and &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; characterization [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31950558"&gt;North20&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
